WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.988
[BEEPING SOUNDS]

00:00:04.755 --> 00:00:06.130
SPEAKER: Google
Kubernetes Engine

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:08.770
provides a managed environment
for deploying, managing,

00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:11.080
and scaling your
containerized applications

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:12.940
using Google infrastructure.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:14.830
The environment
Kubernetes Engine provides

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:18.160
consists of multiple machines,
specifically, Google Compute

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.260
Engine instances
grouped together

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:21.940
to form a container cluster.

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:24.370
Kubernetes draws on the
same design principles

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:26.920
that run popular Google
services and provides

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:28.300
the same benefits--

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.220
automatic management
and scaling,

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.350
rolling updates, and more.

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:34.750
When you run your applications
on a container cluster,

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:37.780
you're using technology based
on Google's 10-plus years

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:41.260
of experience running production
workloads in containers.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:43.110
When you run a Kubernetes
engine cluster,

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:45.820
you also gain the benefit of
advanced cluster management

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:48.370
features that Google
Cloud Platform provides.

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:51.310
These include Google Cloud
Platform's load balancing

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:54.010
for Compute Engine
instances, node pools

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:56.410
to designate subsets of
nodes within a cluster

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:59.350
for additional flexibility,
automatic scaling

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:02.620
of your cluster's node instance
count, automatic upgrades

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:04.720
for your cluster's
node software,

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:08.350
node auto-repair to maintain
node health and availability,

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:11.050
logging and monitoring with
Stackdriver for visibility

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:12.610
into your cluster.

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:14.540
This hands-on lab
shows you how to deploy

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:17.440
a containerized application
with Kubernetes Engine.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:19.990
Next up on our quest
is Container Registry--

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:20.890
Qwik Start.

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:23.340
[MUSIC PLAYING]

